      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to use the information provided about the roots of the polynomial equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\). The roots of the polynomial are 3, -2, and some third root \\(r\\). Therefore, the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\n\\[a(x-3)(x+2)(x-r) = 0.\\]\n\nWe can expand this product to find the coefficients of the polynomial. First, we expand \\((x-3)(x+2)\\):\n\n\\[(x-3)(x+2) = x^2 + 2x - 3x - 6 = x^2 - x - 6.\\]\n\nNext, we multiply this result by \\((x-r)\\):\n\n\\[(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r.\\]\n\nFrom this, we can see that the polynomial is:\n\n\\[ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar.\\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients with the original polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we get:\n\n\\[b = -a(r+1),\\]\n\\[c = a(r-6),\\]\n\\[d = 6ar.\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). First, we substitute the expressions for \\(b\\) and \\(c\\):\n\n\\[b + c = -a(r+1) + a(r-6) = -ar - a + ar - 6a = -7a.\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{-7}.\\]",
