    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that the polynomials roots are \\( 3 \\) and \\(-2\\), we can use the fact that for a polynomial with integer coefficients, the roots must occur in conjugate pairs or real pairs. Here, we are dealing with real coefficients, so one of the roots must be \\(3\\) or \\(-2\\), indicating that the polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\\[\n(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d) = a(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r)\n\\]\nwhere \\(r\\) is the third root. We need to determine the third root \\(r\\). Given that we have two roots and a cubic polynomial, the third root \\(r\\) can be found by examining the coefficients. The polynomial can also be expanded to match the general form \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\) and equate the coefficients with \\(x^3 + \\text{higher order terms}\\).\n\nTo expand \\((x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r)\\):\n\\[\n(x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 - x - 6\n\\]\n\\[\n(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r\n\\]\n\nBy comparison with \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we identify the coefficients:\n\\[\na = a, \\quad b = -a(r + 1), \\quad c = a(r - 6), \\quad d = 6ar\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(\\frac{b + c}{a}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{b + c}{a} = \\frac{-a(r + 1) + a(r - 6)}{a} = \\frac{-ar - a + ar - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-a - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
