      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\) for the given cubic equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) with roots \\(3\\), \\(-2\\), and another root \\(r\\), we can use the properties of the roots and the relationship between the roots and the coefficients of the polynomial.\n\nGiven that \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\) are roots, we can express the polynomial in terms of its roots:\n\\[a(x-3)(x+2)(x-r) = 0.\\]\n\nExpanding this product, we get:\n\\[\na(x-3)(x+2)(x-r) = a(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = a(x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r) = a(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r).\n\\]\nThus, the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar = 0.\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients with the original polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we identify:\n\\[\nb = -a(r+1), \\quad c = a(r-6), \\quad d = 6ar.\n\\]\n\nWe are asked to find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). Substituting the expressions for \\(b\\) and \\(c\\), we get:\n\\[\nb + c = -a(r+1) + a(r-6) = -ar - a + ar - 6a = -7a.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}.\n\\]",
